We parameterize the semicircle $C$ by $z = Re^{i\theta}$, $\theta \in [0, \pi]$, so $dz = iRe^{i\theta}\,d\theta$. Then

$$
\int_C \frac{e^{iz}}{z^2}\,dz = \int_0^\pi \frac{e^{iRe^{i\theta}}}{R^2 e^{2i\theta}}\, iRe^{i\theta}\,d\theta = \frac{i}{R}\int_0^\pi e^{iRe^{i\theta}}\, e^{-i\theta}\,d\theta.
$$

**Bounding the integrand.** Since $iRe^{i\theta} = iR(\cos\theta + i\sin\theta) = -R\sin\theta + iR\cos\theta$, we have

$$
\left|e^{iRe^{i\theta}}\right| = e^{-R\sin\theta},
$$

and $|e^{-i\theta}| = 1$. Therefore

$$
\left|\int_C \frac{e^{iz}}{z^2}\,dz\right| \le \frac{1}{R}\int_0^\pi e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta.
$$

**Estimating the remaining integral.** Using the symmetry $\sin(\pi - \theta) = \sin\theta$,

$$
\int_0^\pi e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta = 2\int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta.
$$

On $[0, \pi/2]$, **Jordan's inequality** gives $\sin\theta \ge \dfrac{2\theta}{\pi}$. Hence $e^{-R\sin\theta} \le e^{-2R\theta/\pi}$, and

$$
