We parametrize the semi-circle \(C\) by \(z = Re^{i\theta}\), \(\theta \in [0,\pi]\), so \(dz = iRe^{i\theta}\,d\theta\). The integral becomes

$$
I(R) = \int_C \frac{e^{iz}}{z^2}\,dz = \int_0^{\pi} \frac{e^{iRe^{i\theta}}}{R^2 e^{2i\theta}}\,iRe^{i\theta}\,d\theta = \frac{i}{R}\int_0^{\pi} e^{iRe^{i\theta}}\,e^{-i\theta}\,d\theta.
$$

**Bounding the integrand.** Write \(Re^{i\theta} = R\cos\theta + iR\sin\theta\), so

$$
e^{iRe^{i\theta}} = e^{iR\cos\theta - R\sin\theta} = e^{-R\sin\theta}\,e^{iR\cos\theta}.
$$

Since \(\left|e^{iR\cos\theta}\right| = 1\) and \(\left|e^{-i\theta}\right| = 1\),

$$
\left|I(R)\right| \le \frac{1}{R}\int_0^{\pi} e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta.
$$

**Estimating the remaining integral.** By symmetry \(\sin(\pi-\theta)=\sin\theta\),

$$
\int_0^{\pi} e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta = 2\int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta.
$$

On \([0,\pi/2]\), Jordan's inequality gives \(\sin\theta \ge \frac{2\theta}{\pi}\), hence \(e^{-R\sin\theta} \le e^{-2R\theta/\pi}\). Therefore

$$
\int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta \le \int_0^{\pi/2} e^{-2R\theta/\pi}\,d\theta = \frac{\pi}{2R}\left(1 - e^{-R}\right) \le \frac{\pi}{2R}.
$$

Combining,

$$
\int_0^{\pi} e^{-R\sin\theta}\,d\theta \le \frac{\pi}{R},
$$

and so

$$
\left|I(R)\right| \le \frac{1}{R}\cdot\frac{\pi}{R} = \frac{\pi}{R^2}.
$$

**Conclusion.** As \(R \to \infty\),

$$
\left|\int_C \frac{e^{iz}}{z^2}\,dz\right| \le \frac{\pi}{R^2} \longrightarrow 0.
$$

$$
\boxed{\lim_{R\to\infty}\int_C \frac{e^{iz}}{z^2}\,dz = 0.}
$$

